            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0249" n="149" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/23880: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Vera Cruz</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Foster</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vera Cruz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-08T08:00:00-06:36"><hi rendition="#italic">May 8, 1920—8
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 1:25 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Conditions growing better. General Guadeloupe Sánchez has the situation well in
                    [hand] and has requested civil authorities to continue functioning and that a
                    reestablishment [omission]. No reprisals will be maintained [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] with the exception of General Aguilar who will
                    be required to resign as Governor of Vera Cruz and leave the country. He has
                    detailed troops for the defense of Vera Cruz against attacks by rebels or
                    bandits. Thanks to the firmness and discretion of Generals Sanchez and Medina
                    there are no incidents of a disagreeable nature to American citizens to report.
                    American warships mentioned in the Department’s telegram May 3, 6 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">1</hi>] p.m. have not arrived and will probably not be
                    necessary unless a change in condition should occur.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Foster</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
